---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import IphoneMockup from '../../components/IphoneMockup.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import BrowserSupport from '../../components/BrowserSupport.astro';
import ResourceList from '../../components/ResourceList.astro';

const browserRows = [
  { browser: 'Safari iOS', version: 'All', supported: 'partial', notes: 'Renders as native drum-wheel picker — not styleable with CSS' },
  { browser: 'Chrome iOS', version: 'All', supported: 'partial', notes: 'Same WebKit engine — same native picker' },
  { browser: 'Chrome Android', version: 'All', supported: 'partial', notes: 'Native Android date picker (Material) — different but also hard to style' },
  { browser: 'Firefox', version: 'All', supported: true, notes: 'Custom styleable date picker; -moz-appearance: none supported' },
  { browser: 'Safari macOS', version: 'All', supported: 'partial', notes: 'Renders inline — minimal styling via appearance:none' },
  { browser: 'Chrome macOS/Windows', version: 'All', supported: true, notes: 'Custom styleable date widget' },
];

const resources = [
  {
    title: 'MDN: <input type="date">',
    url: 'https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Element/input/date',
    description: 'Full reference for the date input type including notes on mobile behaviour.'
  },
  {
    title: 'Can I Use: date input',
    url: 'https://caniuse.com/input-datetime',
    description: 'Browser compatibility for date, time, datetime-local inputs across browsers.'
  },
  {
    title: 'Flatpickr documentation',
    url: 'https://flatpickr.js.org/',
    description: 'Lightweight, dependency-free date picker library with full CSS customisation.'
  },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
  title={'<input type="date"> Inconsistencies'}
  summary="Date and time inputs render as a native iOS date picker wheel on Safari, ignoring CSS styling and behaving differently from desktop or Android."
  affectedSince="All iOS Safari versions"
  slug="date-input-quirks"
>
  <style>
    .mockup-center { display: flex; justify-content: flex-start; margin: 2rem 0; }

    .date-phone {
      position: absolute;
      inset: 0;
      background: #111;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
    }
    .date-content {
      padding: 32px 12px 8px;
      flex-shrink: 0;
    }
    .date-label {
      font-size: 8px;
      color: #888;
      margin-bottom: 4px;
    }
    .date-field {
      background: #2a2a2a;
      border: 1px solid #444;
      border-radius: 8px;
      height: 28px;
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      padding: 0 10px;
      gap: 6px;
      margin-bottom: 8px;
    }
    .date-field-text {
      font-size: 9px;
      color: #ccc;
      font-family: monospace;
    }
    .date-field-icon {
      margin-left: auto;
      color: var(--accent);
    }
    /* iOS drum picker */
    .drum-picker {
      flex: 1;
      background: #1c1c1e;
      border-top: 1px solid #333;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      overflow: hidden;
    }
    .picker-toolbar {
      height: 28px;
      background: #2c2c2e;
      border-bottom: 1px solid #3a3a3a;
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: space-between;
      padding: 0 12px;
    }
    .picker-cancel {
      font-size: 8px;
      color: var(--accent);
    }
    .picker-done {
      font-size: 8px;
      font-weight: 700;
      color: var(--accent);
    }
    .picker-title {
      font-size: 8px;
      font-weight: 700;
      color: #fff;
    }
    .picker-columns {
      flex: 1;
      display: flex;
      position: relative;
    }
    .picker-col {
      flex: 1;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: center;
      gap: 3px;
      overflow: hidden;
    }
    .picker-col-label {
      font-size: 6px;
      color: #666;
      margin-bottom: 2px;
    }
    .picker-item {
      font-size: 9px;
      color: #555;
      padding: 3px 0;
      width: 100%;
      text-align: center;
    }
    .picker-item.selected {
      font-size: 11px;
      font-weight: 700;
      color: #fff;
    }
    .picker-selection-bar {
      position: absolute;
      top: 50%;
      left: 8px;
      right: 8px;
      height: 24px;
      background: rgba(255,255,255,0.05);
      border-top: 1px solid #3a3a3a;
      border-bottom: 1px solid #3a3a3a;
      transform: translateY(-50%);
      pointer-events: none;
      border-radius: 4px;
    }
    .unstyleable-badge {
      position: absolute;
      top: 32px;
      right: 4px;
      background: #ff453a;
      color: #fff;
      font-size: 6px;
      font-weight: 700;
      padding: 2px 5px;
      border-radius: 4px;
      white-space: nowrap;
    }

    .note {
      background: rgba(10,132,255,0.07);
      border: 1px solid rgba(10,132,255,0.2);
      border-radius: 8px;
      padding: 1rem 1.2rem;
      font-size: 0.875rem;
      color: #ccc;
      margin: 1rem 0;
      line-height: 1.6;
    }
    .note strong { color: var(--accent); }
    .tip {
      background: rgba(48,209,88,0.07);
      border: 1px solid rgba(48,209,88,0.2);
      border-radius: 8px;
      padding: 1rem 1.2rem;
      font-size: 0.875rem;
      color: #ccc;
      margin: 1rem 0;
      line-height: 1.6;
    }
    .tip strong { color: #30d158; }
  </style>

  <h2>The Problem</h2>

  <p>
    On iOS Safari, <code>&lt;input type="date"&gt;</code> opens a native <strong>UIDatePicker</strong> — a
    spinning drum-wheel interface that pops up from the bottom of the screen. This is familiar iOS UX, but
    it's completely impossible to style with CSS. Any custom design system that relies on a consistent date
    picker widget across platforms will look different (or broken) on iOS.
  </p>

  <div class="mockup-center">
    <IphoneMockup label="Native iOS drum picker — not styleable">
      <div class="date-phone">
        <div class="date-content">
          <div class="date-label">Check-in date</div>
          <div class="date-field">
            <span class="date-field-text">2026-07-15</span>
            <svg class="date-field-icon" width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 12 12" fill="none">
              <rect x="1" y="2" width="10" height="9" rx="2" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2"/>
              <path d="M4 1v2M8 1v2M1 5h10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-linecap="round"/>
            </svg>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="drum-picker">
          <div class="picker-toolbar">
            <span class="picker-cancel">Cancel</span>
            <span class="picker-title">Select Date</span>
            <span class="picker-done">Done</span>
          </div>
          <div class="picker-columns">
            <div class="picker-col">
              <div class="picker-item">June</div>
              <div class="picker-item">July</div>
              <div class="picker-item selected">Aug</div>
              <div class="picker-item">Sep</div>
              <div class="picker-item">Oct</div>
            </div>
            <div class="picker-col">
              <div class="picker-item">13</div>
              <div class="picker-item">14</div>
              <div class="picker-item selected">15</div>
              <div class="picker-item">16</div>
              <div class="picker-item">17</div>
            </div>
            <div class="picker-col">
              <div class="picker-item">2024</div>
              <div class="picker-item">2025</div>
              <div class="picker-item selected">2026</div>
              <div class="picker-item">2027</div>
              <div class="picker-item">2028</div>
            </div>
            <div class="picker-selection-bar"></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <span class="unstyleable-badge">CSS styling: none</span>
      </div>
    </IphoneMockup>
  </div>

  <h2>Root Cause</h2>

  <p>
    Safari on iOS renders <code>&lt;input type="date"&gt;</code> as a native UIKit component — a
    <code>UIDatePicker</code> in "wheels" style. This is controlled at the OS level and WebKit delegates
    rendering entirely to native UIKit, bypassing CSS styling hooks. The value format is also normalised
    by iOS before being exposed to JavaScript, which can differ from other platforms.
  </p>

  <h2>Option 1 — Accept native behaviour, style the trigger field</h2>

  <p>
    The simplest approach: accept the native picker (it provides good UX for iOS users) and style only the
    input field itself — the visible trigger before the picker opens.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="css"
    filename="date-input.css"
    code={`/* Style the input trigger field (not the picker) */
input[type="date"],
input[type="datetime-local"],
input[type="time"] {
  appearance: none;
  -webkit-appearance: none;
  border: 1px solid #ccc;
  border-radius: 8px;
  padding: 12px 16px;
  font-size: 16px; /* prevent auto-zoom on iOS */
  font-family: inherit;
  width: 100%;
  background: #fff;
  color: #000;
}

/* Focus state */
input[type="date"]:focus {
  outline: none;
  border-color: #007aff;
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px rgba(0, 122, 255, 0.2);
}`}
  />

  <h2>Option 2 — JavaScript date picker library</h2>

  <p>
    For a fully consistent cross-platform UI, use a JS library that suppresses the native picker and renders
    its own calendar. <strong>Flatpickr</strong> is lightweight (~16kb gzipped), zero-dependency, and works
    identically on all platforms.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="html"
    filename="datepicker.html"
    code={`<!-- Flatpickr: fully custom, works on iOS -->
<link rel="stylesheet" href="flatpickr.min.css">
<script src="flatpickr.min.js"><\/script>

<input type="text" id="datepicker" placeholder="YYYY-MM-DD">

<script>
  flatpickr("#datepicker", {
    dateFormat: "Y-m-d",
    allowInput: true,
    // Prevents iOS from showing native keyboard for the text field
    disableMobile: false,
  });
<\/script>`}
  />

  <h2>Option 3 — text input with inputmode</h2>

  <p>
    For simple cases, use a text input with the <code>inputmode</code> attribute to show a numeric keyboard
    and a regex <code>pattern</code> for validation. No picker at all.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="html"
    filename="text-date.html"
    code={`<label for="date">Date (YYYY-MM-DD)</label>
<input
  type="text"
  id="date"
  name="date"
  inputmode="numeric"
  placeholder="2026-07-15"
  pattern="\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}"
  title="Enter a date in YYYY-MM-DD format"
>`}
  />

  <div class="tip">
    <strong>Recommendation by use case:</strong><br />
    Simple forms with familiar UX: <strong>Option 1</strong> (native picker, styled trigger).<br />
    Design-system components needing pixel-perfect cross-platform consistency: <strong>Option 2</strong> (Flatpickr).<br />
    Admin tools or power-user interfaces: <strong>Option 3</strong> (text + inputmode).
  </div>

  <h2>Browser Support</h2>

  <BrowserSupport rows={browserRows} />

  <h2>Further Reading</h2>

  <ResourceList links={resources} />
</ArticleLayout>
